I dare say that, in terms of the final result, all of this may not matter.
The question is, how confident will the university feel in its position,
and how much will it be willing to risk in order to defend that position?

In other words, just the threat of legal action may be sufficient to have
the disciplinary action dropped.

About a year or two ago, The Chronicle of Higher Education ran a letter
(or was it a column?) from a Professor who was aghast at the treatment he
had received from his school's administration over an academic dishonesty
case.  The case was apprently as air-tight as could be--the professor had
several exams of a student whose answers (all of them) were identical
(including the errors) to that of the student sitting in front of him.

(The student was discovered cheating by a proctor, and the Prof. subsequently
checked all of the previous exams...)

In any case,disciplinary proceedings were started against the student, and the
student's father, a lawyer, requested copies of the evidence against his son.
(That is, copies of the exams.)  The professor provided him with copies, and
being ignorant of the law, failed to black out the name of the other student.

The father then warned the school that if the disciplinary action were not
dropped, he would file a complaint against the school for violating FERPA.

Naturally, the school's lawyers advised them that they could have trouble,
and the administration essentially forced the professor to drop the case.
(The professor did send a message to the administration by giving the
student in question an "A.")


The point simply is, high-level college administrators have a strong
tendency to be real weenies that are scared of their own shadows.  Often
times expedience rather than principle will guide their actions.

Even if there is serious question about the merits of the students' claim
(of illegal privacy invasion), I wouldn't be a tad bit surprised to see
the administration try to dispose of the case to avoid problems.


