Okay, so you are opposed to censorship too? Then I will provide you
some arguments against censorship, and tell you how this is compatible
with reasonable child-raising.

> to UseNet groups aimed at K-12 being moderated because you feel
> students should be exposed to everything then make their own
> choice, right ? At least that is the drift I get from reading

Wrong. I'm objecting against Usenet groups being *censored* because I
feel it is important to maintain free flow of information as one of
the fundamental civil liberties. And IMHO fundamental civil liberties
do not end at the entrance to a high school, even when persons are
involved that happen to be under 18 (which is a totally arbitrary
limit and was set only to have a line been drawn *somewhere*.)

Now, the diference between moderation and censorship: Moderation is
something established by *consensus* of the people involved, basically
to cut down on material nobody wants there because it's agreed upon
this material being inappropriate (e.g. discussions in source groups,
or flame-wars in groups devoted to serious discussion).  A newsgroup
will be moderated if it is decided by a voting, i.e. by a democratic
process. (Freedom, e.g. freedom of speech, and democracy are
inseparatably intertwined; none can survive without the other, as
history has shown.) What is discussed about here is a proposal to cut
off certain postings because they contain "bad words", and NOT letting
the people affected (i.e. the high school students) decide upon it
(Otherwise the proposal would be rejected. These students are way more
reasonable than most parents imagine.)

One poster even got far enough to propose the rejection of postings
based on the Newsgroups: line. That is, if I would crosspost this to
alt.sex it would be deleted although there is nothing in it what this
person would consider inappropriate (unless he considers discussion on
censorship and on educating children to be inappropriate, too.) This
has nothing to do with moderation in the usual sense - if I post to a
moderated newsgroup, I take into account the fact that the moderator,
who is known to me, may reject the posting. 

> I have a daughter, 16 months old, who I love more than I thought

The first, and most important, mistake you made is not differentiating
between 16-months-olds and 16-*year*-olds. You will save your daughter
much worry if you start *now* realizing that she will be different
from now in a decade to come. (Don't tell me that's trivial. I learned
that the hard way, and probably you too, see below.)

> possible.  Saterday morning while we are playing, I choose to set
