You are not moderating, you are not censoring in the classical sense
too, you are making a decision your daughter is *yet* unable to do for
herself. Note that I know many people who would consider it totally
impossible to let a 16-months-old watch TV at all.

> daughter (yes, she can work it) and let her decide what to watch,
> or should I go further and require her to watch each channel for
> a time in order to make an "educated" choice?

Which you should definitely do some years from now.

> Rachel is not able to make those choices yet.  I would also

And you conclude that she will not be in 14 1/2 years either. That
seems to be the fundamental error every parent makes. (If you are
interested: I have no children, but I have parents :-/)

And she is not able to take part in USENET discussions now, but she
will be when in final years of high school.

> contend that it is not only my right as a parent, but my
> OBLIGATION, to monitor/censor what she is exposed to each day.

Right, but this is a question of degree. The younger your child is,
the more monitoring and guidance she needs. But the other part of the
lesson, much harder to take for parents, is: the older she gets, the
*less* guidance she will need. Birds don't learn flying in a cage
easily, no matter if the cage is golden.

> [**I** was expose to pornography at an early age, and like most
> children, I could not get enough of it.  I have been fighting

Then you probably did not get reasonable and sufficient education
about these things, which is much more important for future life than
