> good" already.  I do not want my daughter to learn about sex
> over the BBSs for several reasons. First, that's a parent's
> responsibility.  In the absense of the parent, I would much

Than it is YOU who should take the responsibility and not whine about
the all-so-evil BBSs, and the least complain about people who want to
defend civil liberties for adults, including those who are not legally
adults but will soon be. Scapegoating BBSs for the failures of parents
is as inappropriate as scapegoating the press. If you (arguing away
from porn) don't want your daughter to read the "National Enquirer" as
her only source of political information because you think there are
better ones, you can teach her to choose her sources proper, but you
can't simply tell her, "Stay away of the N.E." and expect her to do
just that for the rest of her life. And subsequently, when this
approach has failed, put the blame on the whole press for not giving
your daughter proper education.

> rather her learn about it at church (which isn't going to
> happen).  The schools have been burdened with becoming the 
> parents to our children, but if they are to learn about sex

The church has failed to replace the parents in educating their
children, (at least in this particular point), and the schools will
