I must admit that, in this particular case, I have no problem with
a restricted Usenet feed.  I arrived at this decision after consi-
deration of a few points which haven't really been aired.

   Some net.folks have argued that the "appropriateness" of some 
   Usenet materials may change as the target group changes.  This
   is basically correct; an 15-year-old is probably more prepared
   for "controversial" materials such as soc.bi, alt.sex.*, etc.
   than, say, a 7-year-old.

   HOWEVER, let me ask you this: Would you trust your school(s) to
   make that decision for you?  How would you react if your 5th-grade
   son or daughter came home with hardcopy of, say, the vibrator FAQ?  I
   suspect that your reaction would vary widely, right? I can also freely 
   admit that I would NOT want to be in that admin's shoes if the parent(s)
   did not agree with the teacher's estimate of the child's maturity.

   Therefore, we need to involve parents in this process.  Of course,
   the mere discussion of this material will, in many communities,
   spark a firestorm of protest that (in all probability) will nuke
   the entire feed.  I realize that this sounds like the infamous
   "some is better than none" argument, but it's valid in this
   case.

   Of course, parental consent (or lack thereof) will not stop the
   display/distribution of the material.  How many of us got our
   first glimpse of Playboy from someone else?  When that happens
   (and it WILL happen), we're right back to the "firestorm of protest"
   scenario.  The most common analogy in my experience would be the 
   "more mature" child who told my classmates that "My mommy and daddy 
   said there's no Santa Claus".  Believe it or not, but I can remember 
   tearful children (and some rather angry parents) in that incident.  
   I grew up in rural Kentucky, and I can easily see an unrestricted feed 
   going down the tubes in a wail of parental anguish.

   If an separate feed is set up for older students, do you really think
   that this will solve the problem?  I don't mind telling you that my
   first glimpses of Playboy came while riding a school bus, courtesy 
   of an 8th grader (I was in the 3rd grade).  This won't solve the problem.

To me, it boils down to the following:

       1) Many (if not most) of us acknowledge that younger students are
          not prepared to handle "constroversial" Usenet materials.

       2) There is no effective means to control the distribution of such
          materials in the student population; if some get it, it WILL
          trickle down to the younger students.

       3) There is no effective way to distinguish propriety of a given
          piece of material on a student-by-student basis.  You can't
          expect a teacher to say "You 25 students can look at this, but
          you 5 aren't ready for it yet."  In fact, most parents would
          probably resent any such action by the teacher, regardless of
          their child's placement in the matter.

       4) Given the current state of computing in most K-12 schools, a
          second feed (including the controversial groups) isn't generally
          feasible.  Of course, a second feed would also reintroduce the
          "distribution control" problem mentioned as #2 above.

       5) Since we cannot control the distribution at its endpoint, the
          only logical (or effective) means of control is at the source,
          i.e. the feed site.

I strongly suspect that some participants in this discussion are attempting
to apply university-style academic freedom concepts to the K-12 world; I
don't believe we can operate under that assumption.  The parenting angle
makes such assumptions impossible.  I have known 7-year-olds who were quite
conversant with the "facts of life" and 12-year-olds who, in the estimation
of their parents, weren't ready for that knowledge.  Do we really want (or
expect) a teacher or Usenet admin to make that determination?

Given the constraints of primary (K-8) education, I can accept a restricted
Usenet feed for that environment.  High school (9-12) students are, as many
have written, a different bunch of people; I haven't really considered how
to handle that group.  Since most high schools in the US are separate physi-
cal facilities, we might not have to worry about the "underground distribu-
tion" problem.  Then again, my school buses carried everyone, K through 12.

Most of our (university/college) concepts of academic freedom seem to be 
based on the notion that college/university students are adults.  They are
also based on some powerful generalizations about the academic environment.
I have some difficulty applying those concepts to progressively younger 
groups of people.  With K-12 networking, we are talking about exposing these
students to "controversial" materials at a time in which their morals, ethics,
and perspectives and still (in many, if not most cases) at a formative stage.
For instance, one poster mentioned that his early experience with Playboy
et cetera left him with (in paraphrase) "a lifelong problem with lust which
he still trying to control".  My similar experience (in 3rd grade) left me 
with (apparently) no such problems.  Each child is different at that stage; 
I believe that generalizations are impossible to make.  

[ A brief divergence:  Why don't we see a hue and cry about the minimum ]
[ age for driving?  I've certainly known 12-year-old who could drive as ]
[ well as any 16-year-old, but they still had to wait 4 years for their ]
[ license.  Could it be that we *gasp* accept the wisdom of the state?  ]

If we cannot make the same generalizations, how can we apply the same 
policies/notions/concepts?  I don't believe that we can.  At this stage
of the game (and with the current level of technology), I believe that 
a restricted feed is acceptable; in fact, it might be in the best 
interest of the children.   

{ forecasting mode }

In the future, when networking reaches the home, this situation can be
handled by individual students and their parents.  At that time, I
believe that 'controversial' topics can be handled better with the
younger students.  Until that time, I don't believe that the school
can make suitable judgements about each student's ability to "handle"
these materials.

--Wes

