LIPSCOJA@ctrvax.Vanderbilt.Edu (Andy Lipscomb) writes:

>I don't think this helps.  The group is not one work; it is a collection of
>works (articles).  Besides, Usenet postings are not to my knowledge
>copyrighted (normally).  So what this is doing is not cutting out part of one
>work; it is rejecting entire works, which is a different issue.
[...]

The Copyright Act has provisions for "compilations" and "collective
works". I believe moderated newsgroups qualify.

Also, according to the Copyright Act qualifying works are born
copyrighted; there is no requirement of a copyright notice. Usenet
postings can and are copied via Usenet, not because they are
uncopyrighted, but because the copyright owner implicitly gives
permission for such copying when he or she posts. I don't believe
there is any such implicit permission to expurgate moderated
newsgroups.

As far as unmoderated newsgroups, I can see nothing illegal in
selecting some articles and removing others. I believe, however, such
filtering is intellectually dishonest if the derivative work is put
out under the original's name. By way of analogy, it would not be
illegal to to rewrite the Federalist papers such that the words
"freedom" and "slavery" were swapped. It would, however, be
intellectually dishonest to publish this derivative as "The Federalist
Papers".

- Carl

