We're into an important procedural area here.  Eric is suggesting exactly the
right approach.  Go to the school and raise your concern with the principal. 
Ask, if you want to, that your child's access be removed.  But then listen. 
Your child's principal, if he or she is worth anything, will talk with you
about responsible citizenship, open access to information, responsible guidance
of children and similar matters that parents, teachers, school officials, and
children need to be talking to each other about.  As long as you don't make
your request with a closed mind but will engage in dialog, you're being a part
of a very important process that, unfortunately, not enough parents participate
in.  The result will, hopefully, be a compromise that recognizes both your
values and the school's values, if those are not identical.  Pragmatically,
this is the sort of scenario that should take place. 

