From caf-talk Caf Jan 20 12:07:56 1993
From: s_titz@ira.uka.de (Olaf Titz)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Re: K-12 schools and Usenet feeds
Date: 20 Jan 1993 17:06:58 GMT
Message-ID: <1jk0riINNgsi@nz12.rz.uni-karlsruhe.de>

Wes Morgan has written a very good article, most of which I even don't
consider necessary to comment, although I don't agree. There are only
a few points.

In article <1993Jan19.132749.6573@ms.uky.edu> morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:

>    If an separate feed is set up for older students, do you really think
>    that this will solve the problem?  I don't mind telling you that my
>    first glimpses of Playboy came while riding a school bus, courtesy 
>    of an 8th grader (I was in the 3rd grade).  This won't solve the problem.

You can't stop people who have unrestricted access outside the school
from disseminating any material to students either.

>        2) There is no effective means to control the distribution of such
>           materials in the student population; if some get it, it WILL
>           trickle down to the younger students.

Right, but this probably extends to the world outside the schools too.

>        3) There is no effective way to distinguish propriety of a given
>           piece of material on a student-by-student basis.  You can't

Right.

>        5) Since we cannot control the distribution at its endpoint, the
>           only logical (or effective) means of control is at the source,
>           i.e. the feed site.

The feed is not the source. The source (we've already had this many
times already) is the poster, who can't be controlled either. Another
possibility would be to set up a separate network consisting only of
moderated groups, but this is a completely different thing. (You
wouldn't call this "USENET access" either.)

> I strongly suspect that some participants in this discussion are attempting
> to apply university-style academic freedom concepts to the K-12 world; I
> don't believe we can operate under that assumption.  The parenting angle

For my part, these concepts are *not* invalid; you can't draw a line
between the "outside world" with all maturity and freedom and the
"high-school world" with no maturity and freedom. After all, the high
school students have to develop maturity and learn freedom by
themselves, and they will not if they are treated like little babies
until they are 18, and then expected to get along with the Real World
at once.

> makes such assumptions impossible.  I have known 7-year-olds who were quite
> conversant with the "facts of life" and 12-year-olds who, in the estimation
> of their parents, weren't ready for that knowledge.  Do we really want (or
> expect) a teacher or Usenet admin to make that determination?

No, and **this is exactly why** I oppose a centrally censored feed.

> to handle that group.  Since most high schools in the US are separate physi-
> cal facilities, we might not have to worry about the "underground distribu-
> tion" problem.  Then again, my school buses carried everyone, K through 12.

I doubt. You simply can't separate a school from the rest of the world.

> Most of our (university/college) concepts of academic freedom seem to be 
> based on the notion that college/university students are adults.  They are

What "adult" means is so heavily based on individual factors that you
can't simply assume that university people are over 18, therefore they
can bear everything, and high school people are under 18, therefore
they can't bear anything. If you focus on legal problems you may
legally be right but therefore morally even more wrong.

> [ A brief divergence:  Why don't we see a hue and cry about the minimum ]
> [ age for driving?  I've certainly known 12-year-old who could drive as ]
> [ well as any 16-year-old, but they still had to wait 4 years for their ]
> [ license.  Could it be that we *gasp* accept the wisdom of the state?  ]

I do not believe in the simple "wisdom of the state". Instead, I
accept that there are laws that are so borderline that you'll
certainly find instances where the application of this law is so
unjust that you have to consider it morally wrong, but the law has to
be there for practical reasons. BUT this law has to be reconsidered
when it is time. Now we consider people "adult" at 18 in most
countries around the globe, but in Germany this is in effect since
1973. Before the legal age was 21. This shows that these limits are
neither "God-" nor "wisdom of the State"- given, but they have a
purpose and they have to be changed when this purpose has changed.

I see nothing wrong with issuing a driver's licence to 15 or 16 year
olds (here they have to be 18), *just because they are not 18*. I also
see nothing wrong with providing them access to newspapers or TV, just
because there *could* be something "controversial" in it. And I don't
see a reason why this argument should be wrong in the case of computer
networks. 

> of the game (and with the current level of technology), I believe that 
> a restricted feed is acceptable; in fact, it might be in the best 
> interest of the children.   

And finally, someone uses the right word: acceptable. Not desirable.

> In the future, when networking reaches the home, this situation can be
> handled by individual students and their parents.  At that time, I
> believe that 'controversial' topics can be handled better with the
> younger students.  Until that time, I don't believe that the school
> can make suitable judgements about each student's ability to "handle"
> these materials.

Absolutely right. (But that *contradicts* the proposition of a
censor-feed.) 

Olaf
