s_titz@ira.uka.de (Olaf Titz) wrote:
>
>You can't stop people who have unrestricted access outside the school
>from disseminating any material to students either.
>

Agreed, but the school is absolved from liability in these cases (unless
it happens on school grounds).  This may seem like a rather lame argument,
but it is not.  In recent months, schools have been found liable for failure
to prevent teasing and taunting among grade-school students; it was determined
that such behavior is sexual harassment, and that the school has a responsi-
bility to prevent it.  I can just see the reactions (and the lawsuits) when
some students start "having some fun" with alt.sex.* or *.pictures material.

Were we dealing with college students (or even high school students), they
would be treated as adults; they would be subject to due process.  With 
younger students, the schools take the fall.

>> attempting to apply university-style academic freedom concepts to the K-12 
>> world; I don't believe we can operate under that assumption.  The parenting 
>> angle
>
>For my part, these concepts are *not* invalid; you can't draw a line
>between the "outside world" with all maturity and freedom and the
>"high-school world" with no maturity and freedom. After all, the high
>school students have to develop maturity and learn freedom by
>themselves, and they will not if they are treated like little babies
>until they are 18, and then expected to get along with the Real World
>at once.

Agreed.  Notice that I said "university-style"; I didn't invalidate the whole
idea.  I think that many of our academic freedom concepts are based on a cer-
tain assumption of maturity; we cannot make those assumptions in the K-8 crowd.
I'll certainly agree that high school students are a different story.

>> Do we really want (or
>> expect) a teacher or Usenet admin to make that determination?
>
>No, and **this is exactly why** I oppose a centrally censored feed.
>

So you are in favor of dumping a full feed into K-8 students' laps?
I'd like to see you talking to a parent whose 9-year-old brings home
hardcopy of *.pictures or some of the raunchier alt.sex.* material.

A common refrain in discussion of student due process and "fascist
admins" is that the victims pay for our salaries and systems.  Well,
so do the people who don't want their students to see this material.

We can't please everyone.  Very few things *can* please everyone.

>I doubt. You simply can't separate a school from the rest of the world.

Agreed, but you can separate the school from the activity, eh?

>> Most of our (university/college) concepts of academic freedom seem to be 
>> based on the notion that college/university students are adults.  They are
>
>What "adult" means is so heavily based on individual factors that you
>can't simply assume that university people are over 18, therefore they
>can bear everything, and high school people are under 18, therefore
>they can't bear anything. If you focus on legal problems you may
>legally be right but therefore morally even more wrong.
>

Yes, but <unfortunately> we must concern ourselves first with legality.

>I also
>see nothing wrong with providing them access to newspapers or TV, just
>because there *could* be something "controversial" in it. And I don't
>see a reason why this argument should be wrong in the case of computer
>networks. 

Because the *PARENTS* usually make the decisions with regard to other
media.  You would have a third party (namely, teachers/administrators)
make that decision for their students' parents.  In some areas, such
as science/technology or history, that isn't really difficult; in "close
to the heart" matters such as sexuality, it's a decision that I'd rather
see parents make.

>> of the game (and with the current level of technology), I believe that 
>> a restricted feed is acceptable; in fact, it might be in the best 
>> interest of the children.   
>
>And finally, someone uses the right word: acceptable. Not desirable.
>

Thanks.

>Absolutely right. (But that *contradicts* the proposition of a
>censor-feed.) 

Apparently (according to the discussion here), a "censored" feed is any feed 
that does not contain each and every available group.  I'd guess that over 90% 
of Usenet readers are using a censored feed, if we accept that definition.  
Right?



